{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces\n    33\t\n    34\t| # | Defect | Rule (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    35\t|---|---|---|---|\n    36\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number as the quote writes it (`quote_number`): with currency, scale or percent sign, or the words double, triple or twice. The figure's value equals it, in billions of dollars for money. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) is given in `qualifier`, and none is given when none is written. A written range gives low and high. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    37\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A range input gives a range: low and high, or their midpoint as value. Money is never multiplied by a percentage (use share). Arity is as `operation` states. | `operation` |\n    38\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, the part's forecast figure is at most the whole's: each end of the part's value or range is at most the same end of the whole's. The part's outcome is at most the whole's outcome. | the family header; `outcome_for` |\n    39\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    40\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n    41\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    42\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    43\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n    44\t\n    45\t**Recorded for readers, never refused:**\n    46\t- each estimate's assumption;\n    47\t- each computed figure whose money inputs measure another population, measure or scope than its output;\n    48\t- each growth applied to a base of another period;\n    49\t- each source qualifier.\n    50\t\n    51\t## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n    52\t\n    53\t**figures[]**\n    54\t\n    55\t| Field | Type | Description |\n    56\t|---|---|---|\n    57\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n    58\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n    59\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n    60\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n    61\t| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\n    62\t| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n    63\t| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n    64\t| `value` | number or null | \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\n    65\t| `low`, `high` | number or null | \"A range, low at most high.\" |\n    66\t| `unit` | enum USD bn, %, x | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\" |\n    67\t| `evidence_id` | enum of passage ids, or null | \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\" |\n    68\t| `quote_number` | string or null | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency, scale or percent sign and nothing else, for example '$4.5 billion', '$300 million', '40%', '-3%', 'double'; a written range ('$3–4 billion') in full, given as low and high.\" |\n    69\t| `qualifier` | enum none, more than, over, at least, about, approximately, around, roughly, nearly, almost, up to, less than, below, under, or more, or less; or null | \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    70\t| `inputs` | array of strings or null | \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\" |\n    71\t| `operation` | enum sum, difference, product, ratio, share, growth, apply_growth; or null | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + …; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) = a × b × …, never money by a percentage; ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b; share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change from old to new; apply_growth(base, rate) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). difference, ratio, share, growth and apply_growth take exactly two inputs. A range input gives a range (low and high, or their midpoint as value). The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's; when its inputs are periods of one quantity, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    72\t| `basis_text` | string or null | \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\" |\n    73\t| `rests_on` | array of strings or null | \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\" |\n    74\t| `outcome_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: exactly one figure for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    75\t| `reference_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure from a shown passage, or one computed only from such figures and reported ones. No estimate or forecast anywhere beneath it.\" |\n    76\t\n    77\t**answers[]**\n    78\t\n    79\t| Field | Description |\n    80\t|---|---|\n    81\t| `view_id` | (enum, unchanged) |\n    82\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why, and nothing else.\" |\n    83\t| `rung` | \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\" |\n    84\t| `reference_unavailable` | \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\" |\n    85\t| `reasons`, `why`, `cites`, `pushes`, `theme_pushes` | unchanged; `why`: \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\" |\n    86\t\n    87\t**The family header (user message)**\n    88\t- \"Relations: v8 is part of v4: for each of FY2027 Q3, FY2027 Q4, FY2027, the part's forecast figure may not exceed the whole's, and\n    89\t  a sum or difference never combines the two.\"\n    90\t- \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure that uses the quantity takes it: …\"\n    91\t- \"Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    92\t\n    93\t**The system text:** one change. \"a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario\" becomes \"a quantity the family shares\n    94\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n    95\t\n    96\t## 4. Build\n    97\t\n    98\t- **New files.** `calc_small.py` holds `from_answer(data, family)` (wire to internal, with S6/S7 marker errors), `check`,\n    99\t  `publish_decision`, `evaluate`, `upstream_of` and `key`, and returns `{errors, recorded, published}` as `calc_check` does.\n   100\t  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n   101\t- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n   102\t  format uses `calc_check` and `strip`, so the old run's `results.json` reproduces byte for byte on a copy.\n   103\t- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n   104\t- **Size.** Characters and estimated tokens for the whole request, before and after, per item. The schema is expected to be smaller\n   105\t  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n   106\t\n   107\t**Value × reader.**\n   108\t- `from_answer`'s output is read by `check`, `publish_decision`, `merge`, `card_view` and `normalise`. It uses the internal names\n   109\t  those readers read (`evidence`, `inputs`, `rests_on`, `outcome`, `reference`), and the dropped keys never appear.\n   110\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   111\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n   112\t\n   113\t**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n   114\t\n   115\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   116\t\n   117\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   118\t\n   119\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   120\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   121\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   122\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   123\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   124\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   125\t\n   126\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   127\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   128\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   129\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   130\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   131\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   132\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   133\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   134\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   135\t  - \"-3%\";\n   136\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   137\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   138\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   139\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   140\t  - a computed external reference;\n   141\t  - an unresolved member;\n   142\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   143\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   144\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   145\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   146\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   147\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   148\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   149\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   150\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   151\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   152\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   153\t    a reason; neither.\n   154\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   155\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   156\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   157\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   158\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   159\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   160\t  - unit spellings;\n   161\t  - value with range;\n   162\t  - a four-input difference;\n   163\t  - money × % through product;\n   164\t  - \"first half\";\n   165\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   166\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   167\t\n   168\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   169\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   170\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   171\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   172\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   173\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   174\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   175\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   176\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   177\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   178\t  be supported in one reply.\n   179\t\n   180\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   181\t- an A1 form refused;\n   182\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   183\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   184\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   185\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   186\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   187\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   188\t\n   189\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   190\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
