], [250.0, 300.0], [None, 250.0]]]\n8810 ['Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA\\'s total revenue be in FY2027?', 'Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.', '', '[Evidence in view under this thesis, 225 items]', '[Not shown: 1955 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]', '- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.', '    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.', '    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.']\n8816 ['Thesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?', 'Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.', '', '[Evidence in view under this thesis, 97 items]', '[Not shown: 1182 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]', '- e109 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in fiscal Q1 2027, equal to 92% of total sales.', '    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales.', '    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e327, e1691, e1931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.']\n296 79\ne2635 Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of the Instinct MI450 GPUs in its Helios system beginning in the first half of 2027 — a deal that could be worth tens of billions of dollars over time.\ne2652 Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\ne2720 Total revenue is expected to be $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\ne3346 For the third quarter, Nvidia expects revenue between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 85.7% to 93.2% from the $57.01 billion reported in the same quarter last year.\ne4899 Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\ne4925 Revenue for fiscal Q2 stood at $96.2 billion, twice as much as the previous year\ne4947 The company’s annualized revenue run rate eclipsed US$65 billion by late July, skyrocketing from approximately US$9 billion at the end of 2025.\ne4948 The company is now projecting US$190 billion to US$200 billion in revenue by 2028 to support the US$2 trillion public price tag.\ne4949 In late August, Anthropic signed a US$35 billion cloud-computing contract with Lambda, a cloud provider backed by NVIDIA.\ne4950 Earlier in August, Anthropic executed a similar US$45 billion agreement with another NVIDIA-backed cloud provider, Nscale, to lease capacity in West Virginia.\ne4951 The startup committed over US$100 billion in April to Amazon Web Services over the next decade, anchored by plans to use more than 1 million Amazon (NASDAQ:AMZN) Trainium2 chips.\ne4992 Three direct customers generated 44% ($78.2 billion) of Nvidia’s $177.8 billion revenue in the first half of fiscal 2027—up from two customers driving 35% a year earlier.\ne5005 The company expects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\ne5052 Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) expects third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\ne5081 Management then guided Q3 to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\ne5096 Recently, JPMorgan analyst Richard Choe issued a rare double-upgrade on IREN after the company signed a roughly US$5.50 billion, five-year partnership with Nvidia tied to its fast-growing AI cloud services platform.\ne5097 Among recent announcements, the US$3.65 billion GPU financing facility stands out alongside the Nvidia deal.\ne5111 Management projects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\ne5181 C4ADS analyzed transactions between 2022 and 2025, and found $13.4 million of Nvidia A100, H100/GH100, and AD102-series GPUs routed through the aforementioned countries, in a \"consistent pattern.\"\ne5217 Second-quarter revenue hit $96.2bn, up 106% year on year.\ne5218 Data centre revenue more than doubled to $89bn\ne5223 First, it guided total revenue for the next quarter to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said growth would be driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business.\ne5300 the company forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\ne5314 Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues to reach $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\nold-3 [('v6', 9043)] {'v6': {'question': 'How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?', 'ladder': ['Combined spending is below $500 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $800 billion.']}} [[[None, 500.0], [500.0, 600.0], [600.0, 700.0], [700.0, 800.0], [800.0, None]]]\n9043 ['Thesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?', 'Ladder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.', '', '[Evidence in view under this thesis, 50 items]', '[Not shown: 101 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]', '- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.', '    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .', '- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.']\n50 41\ne2458 The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\ne2759 With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\ne4564 The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\ne4766 The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\ne4790 Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\ne5081 The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\ne5105 Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\ne5114 top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\ne5358 Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\ne5376 Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\nold-2 [('v29', 8507)] {'v29': {'question': \"How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\", 'ladder': ['Commitments are below $25 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $100 billion.']}} [[[None, 25.0], [25.0, 50.0], [50.0, 75.0], [75.0, 100.0], [100.0, None]]]\n8507 ['Thesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA\\'s committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA\\'s equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?', 'Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.', '', '[Evidence in view under this thesis, 30 items]', '[Not shown: 52 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]', '- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.', '    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.', '- e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.']\n30 0\ne4668 Anthropic is negotiating to secure NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA) as an anchor investor for a record-breaking initial public offering (IPO) that could value the startup at roughly US$2 trillion.\ne4669 According to a Reuters exclusive , the chipmaker is weighing a US$10 billion commitment to the offering, serving as an early backer that could signal strong institutional confidence to public markets preparing to absorb the astronomical valuations and capital demands of frontier artificial intelligence labs.\ne4729 Nvidia spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of the fiscal year, up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier.\nold-3 [('v41', 9071)] {'v41': {'question': 'What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?', 'ladder': ['FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.']}} [[[50.0, None], [40.0, 50.0], [30.0, 40.0], [20.0, 30.0], [None, 20.0]]]\n9071 ['Thesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?', 'Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.', '', '[Evidence in view under this thesis, 15 items]', \"- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\", '    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.', '    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.', '- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.']\n15 0\ne3660 The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\ne4764 Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\ne4856 with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\ne4857 Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\nroughly US$5.50 billion [{'text': '$5.50 billion', 'form': 'money', 'start': 10, 'value': 5.5, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\n$96.2bn [{'text': '$96.2bn', 'form': 'money', 'start': 0, 'value': 96.2, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\nroughly twice []\n$3–4 billion [{'text': '$3–4 billion', 'form': 'range', 'start': 0, 'low': 3.0, 'high': 4.0, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\nbetween $3 billion and $4 billion [{'text': 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', 'form': 'range', 'start': 0, 'low': 3.0, 'high': 4.0, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\n−3% [{'text': '3%', 'form': 'percent', 'start': 1, 'value': 3.0, 'bound': None, 'unit': '%'}]\n-$4.5 billion [{'text': '$4.5 billion', 'form': 'money', 'start': 1, 'value': 4.5, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\n$4.5 billion ± 3% [{'text': '$4.5 billion ± 3%', 'form': 'relative', 'start': 0, 'midpoint': 4.5, 'relative': 3.0, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\nabout $5 billion and $5 billion [{'text': 'about $5 billion', 'form': 'qualified', 'start': 0, 'bound': 'approx', 'value': 5.0, 'unit': 'USD bn'}, {'text': '$5 billion', 'form': 'money', 'start': 21, 'value': 5.0, 'unit': 'USD bn'}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
